       "en": "on the OTTOMAN ABSENCE in the Ionian islands. Corfu was never Ottoman: it withstood the sieges of 1537, 1571, and 1716 under Venetian rule, which lasted from 1386 to 1797, and later passed into British protectorate status between 1815 and 1864. This means the Ionian islands had a landed aristocracy registered in a Libro d'Oro, an Italian-speaking elite, a minority Catholicism, and a relationship with European modernity entirely different from that of the mainland. The comparison with Peru is fertile: a creole elite culturally oriented outward, with a distinctive language and consumption patterns, governing a peasant population whose language and religion were different. Listen, in Corfu, to what is said about \"those from the mainland,\" and who says it."
