       "en": "In Corfu, the central object is SAINT SPYRIDON. The saint's relic arrived on the island in 1456, brought from Constantinople by refugees after the fall, and his mummified body is processed four times a year through the old town. It is the heart of Corfiot identity: he is credited with having saved the island from plague and from Ottoman sieges, and an overwhelming proportion of the island's men are named Spiros. Watch who takes part in the procession and who merely watches, how the confraternity is organized, which families hold precedence. It is the best ethnography available on how the cult of a saint organizes a community's memory and hierarchy, and it will be recognizable to anyone who has worked on the Lord of Miracles or an Andean patron-saint feast."
