      "This is the end of the first block. Let us retrace the thread we have built over eight entries, because it is worth holding as a structure before entering the Ottoman world. First: a geography of maritime nodes that makes autarky impossible and dependence necessary (entry 1). Second: political communities of extremely high participatory intensity resting on fragile states, which destroy one another and fail to federate (entries 1 and 2). Third: absorption by an external power that respects civic forms while emptying out sovereignty (entries 3 and 4). Fourth: the conversion of one's own past into heritage administered by others, with simultaneous veneration and contempt (entry 5). Fifth: a religious and state identity that later nationalism will have to rewrite (entry 6). Sixth: an insular periphery with a Western colonial structure, distinct from the mainland (entry 7). Seventh: defeat metabolized into operative nostalgia (entry 8). The five blocks that follow are variations on these seven elements."
