       "en": "In Athens, look for the Ottoman layer, which is there and almost entirely unmarked. The Tzistarakis Mosque, from 1759, in Monastiraki, today a museum annex. The Fethiye Mosque, from the seventeenth century, in the Roman Agora, built over a Christian basilica that itself stood over Roman structures. The remains of the medrese beside the Tower of the Winds. And the Bath of the Winds, the city's only surviving hammam, on Kyrristou Street. Notice the museographic treatment: few panels, little context, no narrative that integrates those four centuries into the story of the city. Compare it with the interpretive abundance devoted to the classical period. That asymmetry is the politics of memory in action, and it is exactly equivalent to the way many Peruvian museums treat the colonial period as a mere transit between Inca splendor and the republic."
